{
  "slots": {
    "question": [
      {
        "claim": "Can political language be used to measure partisan polarization at the level of a particular topic, place, and time?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We introduce a methodological framework to reliably measure polarization in public discourse at fine-grained resolution,\nleveraging the vast quantity of human-generated text available on digital platforms.",
            "page": 3
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Were discussions of lockdowns, masks, and vaccines more polarized in conservative states and provinces than in liberal ones?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "This pattern is visible in the polarization rankings for American states and Canadian provinces (Fig 9 and Fig 10), with rankings applied separately to each topic (lockdowns, masks, vaccines) and aggregated as\nan “overall” score.",
            "page": 13
          },
          {
            "text": "the ideological leanings of different regions;",
            "page": 3
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Which political or public health events coincided with peaks in polarization, and how quickly did those peaks form and subside?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We then examine temporal patterns of polarization at the national level in both countries, connecting\npeaks in polarization to specific events and analyzing correlations with vaccination data and conspiracy-related content.",
            "page": 13
          },
          {
            "text": "To evaluate the overall responsiveness of our measure to polarizing events, we computed\naverage polarization scores across topics (Fig 14A,B) and performed event-triggered averaging.",
            "page": 19
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Did regional polarization about vaccines correspond to subsequent vaccination uptake?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Next, we explore the relationship between vaccine-related polarization and actual vaccination rates\nacross US states.",
            "page": 13
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Did days with more discussion of conspiracy theories also have more polarized debate about health interventions?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Finally, we explored the relationship between conspiracy discourse and polarization using time-resolved measurements\nof conspiracy-related tweets compared with aggregate daily polarization.",
            "page": 20
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "central_claim": [
      {
        "claim": "Comparing how opposing partisans discuss the same issue yields a scalable indicator of polarization that can be tracked across places, dates, and issues, rather than just a national score.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "While our study focuses on the COVID-19 context, the proposed\nmethodology for quantifying partisan polarization at scale can be adapted to examine large-scale discussions across\nvarious temporal, spatial, and topical dimensions.",
            "page": 3
          },
          {
            "text": "we describe our method for classifying users as conservative or liberal and justify\nour choice of topic-conditioned language dissimilarity as a proxy for partisan polarization.",
            "page": 3
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Pandemic debate did not divide partisans uniformly. The extent of division depended on the intervention, the region’s politics, and the events around which users were responding.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "By analyzing millions of tweets and\ntracking semantic differences between partisan groups across different regions and topics, we have demonstrated that\npolarization is not uniform but varies significantly by geography, political leaning, and public health topic, with substantial\ndifferences between the two countries.",
            "page": 25
          },
          {
            "text": "Our study provides new insights by demonstrating that partisan divisions correlate with regional heterogeneity in social\nmedia discourse, particularly during salient political events concerning health measures.",
            "page": 23
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The stronger U.S. relationship between online division and later vaccine uptake suggests that a shared media platform does not erase the importance of local political institutions for health behavior.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "while both countries experienced social media\npolarization, the U.S. showed stronger correlations between polarization, conspiracy theories, and public health outcomes\nsuch as vaccination rates. This suggests that the impact of online polarization on public health behaviors may be mediated by broader political and institutional factors.",
            "page": 25
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "evidence": [
      {
        "claim": "The authors sampled real-time Twitter posts from October 9, 2020, to January 4, 2021. Filtering produced about 12.6 million U.S. and 46.6 million Canadian tweets from users sharing COVID-19 content.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We used Twitter’s (X) official API to collect 1% of real-time tweets for the United States and\nCanada from October 9, 2020 to January 4, 2021.",
            "page": 4
          },
          {
            "text": "This process yielded 12,552,213 tweets from 2,657,355 users for the United States and 46,636,206 tweets from\n1,757,675 users for Canada that shared COVID-19-related content.",
            "page": 5
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The three interventions were chosen to cover different kinds of action: restrictions on groups, individual precautions, and a medical intervention.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Our analysis focused on three specific pandemic-related topics: lockdowns, masks, and vaccines. These topics were\nselected for their salience in polarized discourse [27,43,44], and because they represent different types of interventions\n(group behavior, individual behavior, and medicine, respectively).",
            "page": 4
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Political scientists labeled hashtags and reviewed sampled tweets. The authors used these labels to train and validate country-specific RoBERTa topic classifiers, giving less weight to hashtag-derived labels.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Two political scientists manually annotated this list with topic and relevance labels, narrowing it to 631 relevant\nhashtags.",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "Two political scientists independently reviewed each tweet to determine relevance, discarding\ntweets where consensus couldn’t be reached. We then fine-tuned the respective domain-adapted RoBERTa models to\nclassify by topics.",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "During training, we also incorporated hashtag-annotated tweets as weak supervision, assigning them a reduced loss weight of 0.9 compared to 1.0 for\ngold-standard labels.",
            "page": 5
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The conspiracy measure counted discussion, not belief: it included posts refuting a theory as well as posts promoting it, and its labels could overlap with the intervention topics.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "For\nconspiracy-related tweets, relevant indicates that the content pertains to COVID-19 conspiracy theories, including tweets\nthat either promote the conspiracy or debunk / refute it, i.e., any tweet discussing the theory in either context. A tweet\ncould belong to multiple topics.",
            "page": 5
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The authors resolved profile locations with two mapping services and checked the geographic distribution against census counts; the regional count correlations were 0.98 in the U.S. and 0.92 in Canada.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We established a clear geolocation when both APIs returned coordinates\nwithin one degree of each other, finding this approach more accurate than using pre-trained Named Entity Recognition\nalgorithms.",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "In total, we successfully geolocated 757,601 U.S. users with a strong correlation of 0.98 (n = 52, p = 9.27e-35, CI=[0.96,\n0.99]) to official population census data, and 282,454 Canadian users with a strong correlation of 0.92 (n = 13, p = 6.20e06, CI=[0.76, 0.98]).",
            "page": 6
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Partisan labels came first from politically explicit profiles and then from a classifier trained on the language of those users’ posts.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We first identified politically explicit users based on their profile descriptions, using partisan keywords for each country’s major parties.",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "For the activity classifier, we generated user embeddings by aggregating tweet embeddings\nfrom each user (using mean pooling), then trained a multi-layer perceptron to predict party affiliation.",
            "page": 8
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The U.S. comparison was between Democrats and Republicans; the Canadian comparison combined three parties on the left and two on the right.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "For the United States, we identified supporters of the Democratic (left) and Republican (right) parties. For Canada, we\ngrouped users into left (Liberal Party, New Democratic Party, and Green Party) and right (Conservative Party and People’s\nParty) partisan families.",
            "page": 4
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The regional ratio of inferred partisans correlated about 0.8 with election results in each country. An individual-level U.S. check against voter records gave lower accuracies, around 73–74%.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "For the U.S. and Canada, we calculated the correlation between the ratio of liberal to\nconservative-labeled users per region in our dataset compared to official election results, obtaining strong correlations of\n0.802 for the United States and 0.815 for Canada (visualized in Fig 3 and Fig 2).",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "we matched Twitter users with primary voter registration records available for five states (Ohio, New York,\nFlorida, Arkansas, North Carolina) and Washington DC.",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "Our profile classifier achieved 74.35% accuracy and our activity classifier achieved 73.35% accuracy when compared\nto these official records.",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "For each topic, the authors averaged each user’s tweet embeddings and used the C-index to score how distinctly those user representations clustered by party.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We first embed each tweet using our language model. To obtain a user embedding for a given topic, we aggregate all\nof the user’s relevant tweet embeddings by averaging.",
            "page": 10
          },
          {
            "text": "We then measure polarization by quantifying how far apart the language of left and right-leaning users is positioned\nin this embedding space, using the C-index [42], a robust clustering measure.",
            "page": 4
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Repeated subsampling made the distance calculation feasible at scale. Tests on lockdown and vaccine discussions put the approximation error below 0.001 at about 10,000 users.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Our algorithm (Algorithm 1) repeatedly samples a fraction of users, calculates the polarization index on this sample,\nand increases the sample size until the coefficient of variation falls below a specified threshold (e.g., 10 [48]).",
            "page": 11
          },
          {
            "text": "We validated our approximation method on daily lockdown and vaccine tweets. As shown in Fig 4, the absolute error\ndrops dramatically at around 3,000 users and falls below 0.001 at approximately 10,000 users.",
            "page": 11
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Regional rankings used twelve weeks of topic-specific scores. For national event analysis, the authors examined daily scores against both preselected dates and events inferred from the largest peaks.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Ranking of 1 signifies the highest average weekly polarization\nbetween October 11, 2020 to January 3, 2021 (12 weeks).",
            "page": 15
          },
          {
            "text": "Our analysis of daily partisan polarization at the national level (Fig 13) reveals rapid daily fluctuations.",
            "page": 16
          },
          {
            "text": "In particular, we examined two types of events: 1) pre-selected political and\nvaccine-related events (Table 8, shown as vertical lines in Fig 13) and 2) highly polarized events detected through analysis of the highest peaks in polarization (Tables 9 and 10, shown as red circles in Fig 13).",
            "page": 16
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "External health measures included official case and death counts and regional rates of receiving at least two vaccine doses one year after the tweets.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We used officially reported COVID-19 new cases and death data from each country.",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "We used officially reported vaccination rates from one year after our Twitter\ndata collection period, as COVID-19 vaccines were only created and approved at the very end of our initial data\ncollection. The vaccination rates represent those who obtained at least two doses.",
            "page": 5
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "key_findings": [
      {
        "claim": "The association between conservative voting and polarization was clearest for U.S. mask and vaccine debates. It was not significant for U.S. lockdown debates or for the Canadian regional comparisons; Canada also had lower polarization overall.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We find\nstrong, significant correlations in the US between Republican vote share and polarization around masks and vaccines,\nthough not for lockdowns. For Canada, these associations are not statistically significant, partly due to the smaller number\nof regions analyzed.",
            "page": 16
          },
          {
            "text": "Canada, while showing similar patterns, demonstrated significantly lower overall polarization levels.",
            "page": 25
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Regional exceptions mattered: liberal Delaware was unusually polarized, several conservative U.S. states were little polarized, and Quebec stood out for division over vaccines.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Delaware, a liberal state, exhibited unexpectedly high polarization, likely due to strict public health measures\nimplemented by the governor in response to rapidly increasing cases during the first pandemic wave [65–67].",
            "page": 23
          },
          {
            "text": "several conservative states like Arkansas, Alabama, and Idaho showed low polarization levels, possibly due to more unified opposition to restrictive COVID-19 measures like mask mandates [68].",
            "page": 23
          },
          {
            "text": "Our results confirmed that Quebec had the highest\nlevel of partisan division over vaccines, alongside the highest reported COVID-19 incidence in Canada during the first\nand second pandemic waves [89].",
            "page": 24
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "U.S. states with more polarized vaccine discussions subsequently had lower vaccination rates (r = −0.77). Canada did not reproduce this inverse relationship: the estimate after excluding Nunavut was positive and based on only 12 regions.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Using official CDC vaccination data from a comparable period after vaccines became\navailable (October 11, 2021 to January 3, 2022), we confirm a strong negative correlation (r = −0.77, p < 0.001) between\nvaccine polarization and vaccination rates.",
            "page": 16
          },
          {
            "text": "We remove Nunavut as an outlier because of its very small population. The correlation is 0.74 with CI = [0.31, 0.92] (n = 12, p = 0.004).",
            "page": 26
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Polarization rose and fell over days rather than remaining elevated. Peaks coincided with events as different as Biden’s vaccination and a Toronto protest against health measures.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "This aggregate analysis (Fig\n14c) confirms a rapid, symmetric rise-and-decay profile around polarization peaks, occurring on timescales of days.",
            "page": 19
          },
          {
            "text": "The second-largest\npeak occurred on December 22, 2020, the day after Biden received his first COVID-19 vaccine [54].",
            "page": 18
          },
          {
            "text": "In Canada’s lockdown discourse (Fig 13B), the highest peak occurred on October 17, 2020, coinciding with a large\nToronto anti-mask protest against COVID-19 measures.",
            "page": 18
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Conservatives discussed conspiracy theories more often in both countries, but higher daily conspiracy volume did not mean greater partisan separation. The association was slightly negative in the U.S. and not significant in Canada.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "conservative partisans in both countries tweet conspiracy-related content at higher rates than progressive\npartisans.",
            "page": 21
          },
          {
            "text": "When correlated with aggregate daily polarization (Fig 15 B and D, we find a small but significant negative correlation\nfor the United States, while no significant correlation was observed for Canada.",
            "page": 21
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "scope_and_limits": [
      {
        "claim": "The score measures semantic separation, not political attitudes directly. Averaging by user can hide sarcasm, indirect disagreement, and other distinctions within a person’s tweets.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "we measured partisan polarization only through the proxy of semantic similarity, which may in certain cases obscure signals not captured by semantic\nembedding representations.",
            "page": 24
          },
          {
            "text": "Because our measure aggregates tweets into user-level embeddings, it captures\noverall partisan alignment at the user level, rather than fine-grained nuances in individual tweets. As a result, subtle rhetorical strategies such as sarcasm or indirect disagreement may be less apparent, while consistent linguistic cues, including negation, are preserved in the aggregated representation.",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The Canadian results do not resolve differences among parties within each left or right family. The classifier confused those parties, and Bloc Québécois and other minor-party supporters were excluded.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Specifically for Canada, we found that the MLP could not distinguish the parties sufficiently – the F1-score was not\nsatisfactory as parties within the liberal (left) party family and conservative (right) party family was easily confused as\nshown in the confusion matrix in Table 5.",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "We also\nremoved supporters of other minor parties and the Bloc Quebecois.",
            "page": 8
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Inferred affiliation is not a verified current party identity: self-descriptions can be inaccurate, registration records can be stale, and name matching can miss users.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Additionally, our classification into partisan groups relies\non self-reported information, which may not be entirely accurate.",
            "page": 24
          },
          {
            "text": "voter registrations may be outdated or not fully reflect\ncurrent partisan leanings expressed online.",
            "page": 10
          },
          {
            "text": "name-based matching can miss users due to nicknames, misspellings, or common names;",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The findings characterize politically engaged Twitter users, not the distribution of opinion in either national population.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Twitter users are more politically engaged and often more partisan than the general population, which means our results reflect dynamics within a vocal subset of online political discourse.",
            "page": 24
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "English-only sampling may distort the Canadian comparison, especially the estimate for predominantly French-speaking Quebec.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "we restricted\nour analysis to English-language tweets, which in Canada means we primarily captured perspectives of either anglophones or bilingual francophones — a potential source of bias. For example, the high polarization observed in Quebec\nregarding COVID-19 measures may be influenced by this language limitation.",
            "page": 24
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Regional assignment is fallible because users supply their own locations; inaccurate locations, VPNs, or fake accounts can affect the geographic data.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "self-reported Twitter locations may be incomplete, imprecise, or even intentionally false\n(including the rare influence of VPNs);",
            "page": 10
          },
          {
            "text": "Furthermore, the use of VPNs or fake accounts (including bots) may introduce additional error.",
            "page": 25
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Bots could exaggerate the measured divide by producing or amplifying unusually partisan language.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Automated accounts can contribute disproportionately to partisan discourse by generating large volumes of ideologically\nslanted content or amplifying existing narratives, potentially inflating the apparent semantic distance between political\ngroups.",
            "page": 25
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The countries were sampled with different keyword strategies. The U.S. emphasis on elections and the Canadian emphasis on provinces and COVID-19 could account for part of the observed contrast.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The U.S. keyword\nlist was dominated by election-related terms, then filtered for COVID-19 terms. By contrast, the Canadian keyword list\nincorporated province names and explicit COVID-related identifiers, which likely offered stronger pandemic relevance.",
            "page": 25
          },
          {
            "text": "This asymmetry may have contributed to cross-national differences in patterns of observed polarization, and this should\nbe considered when interpreting the comparative results.",
            "page": 25
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Late-2020 discourse and later uptake were not measured contemporaneously. Their correlation does not establish that the tweets caused vaccination decisions rather than reflecting other regional differences.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "there are temporal mismatches between our Twitter dataset (collected in late 2020) and regional vaccination rates, which occurred primarily in 2021. As a result, correlations between polarization in vaccine-related discussions\nand later vaccination uptake should be understood as suggestive rather than causative.",
            "page": 25
          },
          {
            "text": "While the exact causal mechanism remains\nunclear, factors such as education and political ideology, which also have strong geographic dependencies, likely played\nsignificant roles [ 60].",
            "page": 23
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The small number of Canadian regions precludes a firm interpretation of the Canadian vaccine-uptake result.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "While we get strong positive correlation with vaccination rate,\nit is over a relatively low number of points.",
            "page": 26
          },
          {
            "text": "However, with the few points, we do not have\na definite conclusion for this result.",
            "page": 26
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Matching a peak to an election, protest, or announcement does not prove that the event caused it.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "While direct causal evidence is not available, we find that many of the largest polarization peaks coincide with\nhighly contentious events specific to each country’s context.",
            "page": 17
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The study does not identify one mechanism explaining why conservative regions were often more divided.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "While our analysis did not identify a single explanation for the relationship between conservative politics and higher\npolarization, state-specific trajectories provide insights.",
            "page": 23
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The influence of cross-border information flows and the size of social networks’ contribution to polarization and health outcomes remain unresolved.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Additionally, the role of cross-border\ninformation flows in shaping polarization deserves further investigation, especially as online communities increasingly\ntranscend national boundaries while still being influenced by local political contexts.",
            "page": 26
          },
          {
            "text": "quantifying\ntheir precise impact on polarization and subsequent public health outcomes remains an important challenge for future\nwork.",
            "page": 26
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "theoretical_positioning": [
      {
        "claim": "The authors extend research on partisan pandemic responses: instead of stopping at the association with conservative politics, they examine where that association holds and when particular events bring divisions to the surface.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "In line with previous research ((e.g., [17,58–60])), we found that more right leaning states and provinces exhibited\ngreater partisan divisions around COVID-19 on Twitter (X), particularly concerning mask mandates and vaccine\ndistribution. However, we extended beyond these studies to characterize the geographic heterogeneity and temporal\nevolution of polarization, connecting features in our polarization metric to real-world events.",
            "page": 22
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Within computational research on polarization, the authors adopt semantic text embeddings rather than inferring ideology from network ties. Their reason is access and scale, not a claim that network methods are invalid.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "At the same time, network-based approaches such as inferring ideology\nfrom follower or retweet structures have proven highly effective in prior work [37,38,39].",
            "page": 3
          },
          {
            "text": "However, access to user- and\nnetwork-level data has become increasingly restricted, particularly under recent platform policy changes. Consequently,\nour study adopts a strictly text-based method, which remains both accessible and scalable for analyzing large-scale digital\ndiscourse.",
            "page": 3
          },
          {
            "text": "Our methodology leverages the demonstrated capacity of modern vector embeddings to represent language semantics.",
            "page": 4
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The authors qualify the premise that different language signals different political positions. Because social groups have distinct vocabularies, they argue that semantic distance is more informative when the topic is held constant.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Therefore, dissimilar language alone does not necessarily indicate polarized\npositions.",
            "page": 4
          },
          {
            "text": "However, when examining discussions on the same contentious topic, language dissimilarity between different partisan\ngroups more likely reflects alternative semantic understandings of that specific topic—a phenomenon we hypothesize\ncorrelates strongly with polarization.",
            "page": 4
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "interlocutor_claim": [
      {
        "claim": "The authors attribute to D. Albrecht the finding that greater Republican vote share is associated with lower vaccination rates. They build on it by showing that a different regional measure—partisan separation in vaccine discussions—also correlates negatively with U.S. uptake.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Importantly, we observe that vaccine polarization is strongly negatively correlated with actual vaccination rates\nacross American states (Fig 12). This aligns with the well-documented negative correlation between Republican vote\nshare and vaccination rates [50].",
            "page": 16
          },
          {
            "text": "Albrecht D. Vaccination, politics and COVID-19 impacts.",
            "page": 29
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
